Scotty Debian source package
============================

The package intentionally has three ownership boundaries:

* scotty owns the application, its private Nearby shared library, desktop and
  AppStream metadata, D-Bus activation file, icons, and dormant user unit.
* gnome-shell-extension-scotty owns only the optional GNOME Shell extension.
* scotty-bluez-compat owns the system-wide BlueZ, hotspot-unit, and Polkit
  integration. Removing it reverses those package-owned system changes.

The user service and GNOME extension are not enabled from maintainer scripts.
D-Bus activation starts Scotty on demand. A user who wants background receiving
at every login can explicitly run:

  systemctl --user enable --now scotty.service

Bazel dependency vendoring
--------------------------

Debian and Launchpad builds cannot access the network. Upstream currently uses
Bzlmod and several remote archives, so an uploadable source package must include
a repository cache produced from the exact locked dependency graph and invoke
Bazel with downloads disabled. The current debian/rules is the local/native
package build and deliberately does not pretend this remaining release job is
complete. Do not upload to a PPA until the dependency URLs are pinned by digest,
the cache is generated as part of the source tarball, and a clean offline build
has passed.

For package-layout testing only, a developer who already built the exact source
tree can skip rebuilding the Nearby library:

  SCOTTY_USE_PREBUILT_NEARBY=1 dpkg-buildpackage -b -d -uc -us

This switch is never appropriate for a release or PPA source build.

For Ubuntu PPA versions, append an Ubuntu suffix to the Debian version, for
example 0.1.0~beta1-1~ubuntu26.04.1, and update debian/changelog with dch.
